### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Refund API — REFUND A PAID ORDER (POST /api/v1/orders/{publicId}/refunds)
#
# How to run: VS Code REST Client — click "Send Request" top to bottom.
#
# Prerequisites:
#   - Docker stack up WITH THE REFUND BRANCH BUILT:
#       docker compose up -d --build --force-recreate pos-backend
#     (V19 must have applied — a pre-refund container 404s the endpoint.)
#   - Seeded chain admin exists: admin@devtunde.com / Password123!
#   - For case 35 only: PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY in .env (sandbox).
#   - For cases 37-40: MONNIFY_API_KEY/MONNIFY_SECRET_KEY in .env (sandbox).
#
# Contract (ticket 0019):
#   - One operation: refund. NO void. Post-sale service act — no session.
#   - Whole order lines only; each line refundable ONCE (database guard);
#     multiple refunds per order are legal.
#   - Money = the FROZEN line totals in kobo (never re-priced).
#   - Gate: the store's MANAGER, the seat admin, or the chain admin.
#     CASHIER excluded — including the selling cashier.
#   - Gateway-paid orders: reversed through the ORIGINAL provider
#     (providerRefundReference rides the response; no returnMethod).
#   - Counter-settled orders: manual return — returnMethod CASH |
#     BANK_TRANSFER is REQUIRED and recorded.
#   - Stock: RESTOCKED (default) returns units with a REFUND movement;
#     DAMAGED writes no movement (spoilt goods leave the shelf).
#   - REFUNDED is DERIVED: every line refunded → the machine view says
#     REFUNDED/FULLY_REFUNDED; the order row itself stays PAID.
#   - The customer receipt is the sale's document — unchanged by refunds.
#
# `# Expected:` labels the status each request proves.
# Tokens expire after 15 MINUTES — re-send the setup from the top.
# Captures are session-scoped, and STALE ONES ARE DANGEROUS: re-sending
# one case (e.g. 33) after a newer setup makes its variables point at a
# DIFFERENT run's store — the API then answers 400 ("Product ... is not
# available in this store") with NO warning. Safe pattern: close this
# tab, reopen it (clears every capture), send 1 -> 36 in one sitting.
# All data is FAKE (devtunde.com emails, Nigerian numbers).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@baseUrl = http://localhost:4000

### 1 — Setup: chain admin logs in
# @name adminLogin
# Expected: 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 2 — Setup: mint the store's seat admin (fresh persona per run)
# @name seatSignup
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Probe Refund Seat Admin",
  "email": "pay-refund-seat-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348091234591"
}

### 3 — Setup: seat admin logs in
# @name seatLogin
# Expected: 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{seatSignup.response.body.user.email}}",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 4 — Setup: chain admin creates the store, seating that admin
# @name store
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/stores
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "brand": "Refund Probe Store {{$timestamp}}",
  "storeType": "KIOSK",
  "storeAdminPublicId": "{{seatLogin.response.body.user.publicId}}",
  "contact": {
    "address": "Probe Way",
    "phone": "08012345691",
    "email": "pay-refund-store-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com"
  }
}

### 5 — Setup: the seat admin hires the store's MANAGER (refund authority)
# @name hireManager
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/stores/{{store.response.body.publicId}}/employees
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Probe Refund Manager",
  "email": "pay-refund-manager-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348091234594",
  "jobTitle": "MANAGER"
}

### 6 — Setup: the manager logs in
# @name managerLogin
# Expected: 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{hireManager.response.body.user.email}}",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 7 — Setup: the seat admin hires the primary cashier (the seller)
# @name hire
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/stores/{{store.response.body.publicId}}/employees
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Probe Refund Cashier",
  "email": "pay-refund-cashier-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348091234592",
  "jobTitle": "CASHIER"
}

### 8 — Setup: the cashier logs in
# @name cashierLogin
# Expected: 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{hire.response.body.user.email}}",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 9 — Setup: the cashier opens a session
# @name session
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/sessions
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 10 — Setup: chain admin creates a category
# @name category
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/categories
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Refund Probe Category {{$timestamp}}"
}

### 11 — Setup: the seat admin lists the milk (₦12,000.00 selling price)
# @name listing
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/products/store/{{store.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Probe Milk 1L",
  "sku": "PROBE-REF-{{$timestamp}}",
  "description": "Refund probe item",
  "brand": "TestBrand",
  "image": "https://cdn.devtunde.com/item.png",
  "categoryPublicId": "{{category.response.body.publicId}}",
  "mrp": 15000.00,
  "sellingPrice": 12000.00
}

### 12 — Setup: stock the milk shelf (10 units)
# @name stock
# Expected: 201 — an OPENING movement is recorded
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/stores/{{store.response.body.publicId}}/inventory
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}",
  "quantity": 10,
  "lowStockThreshold": 1
}

### 13 — Setup: list a second product — the bread (₦4,000.00)
# @name listingBread
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/products/store/{{store.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Probe Bread",
  "sku": "PROBE-REF-B-{{$timestamp}}",
  "description": "Refund probe item two",
  "brand": "TestBrand",
  "image": "https://cdn.devtunde.com/item.png",
  "categoryPublicId": "{{category.response.body.publicId}}",
  "mrp": 5000.00,
  "sellingPrice": 4000.00
}

### 14 — Setup: stock the bread shelf (10 units)
# @name stockBread
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/stores/{{store.response.body.publicId}}/inventory
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productPublicId": "{{listingBread.response.body.publicId}}",
  "quantity": 10,
  "lowStockThreshold": 1
}

### 15 — Setup: hire a SALES_ATTENDANT (the other 403 persona)
# @name hireAttendant
# Expected: 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/stores/{{store.response.body.publicId}}/employees
Authorization: Bearer {{seatLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Probe Refund Attendant",
  "email": "pay-refund-attendant-{{$timestamp}}@devtunde.com",
  "password": "Password123!",
  "phone": "+2348091234593",
  "jobTitle": "SALES_ATTENDANT"
}

### 16 — Setup: the attendant logs in
# @name attendantLogin
# Expected: 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{hireAttendant.response.body.user.email}}",
  "password": "Password123!"
}

### 17 — Setup: the cashier sells one milk, cash
# @name cashOrder
# Expected: 201 — status PAID, totalKobo 1200000
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}",
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ],
  "paymentMethod": "CASH"
}

### 18 — THE HAPPY PATH: the manager refunds the milk, cash back
# Expected: 201 — totalKobo 1200000 (the FROZEN line total),
# displayTotal "₦12000.00", returnMethod "CASH", providerRefundReference null, one line with
# disposition "RESTOCKED". Stock back on the shelf (9 → 10).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 19 — The same line a second time — the database guard says no
# Expected: 409 — refund-line-already-refunded
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 20 — Machine receipt after the refund: the sale is now fully refunded
# Expected: 200 — status "REFUNDED", statusReason "FULLY_REFUNDED"
# (DERIVED — the order row is still PAID), refunds array with ONE entry:
# totalKobo 1200000, displayTotal "₦12000.00", returnMethod "CASH", providerRefundReference null,
# refundedAt present. Read by the SERVING operator (the manager has no
# receipt access — that door is the operator/seat-admin/chain-admin gate).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 21 — Customer receipt after the refund: the sale's document is untouched
# Expected: 200 — total "₦12000.00", NO refunds field
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/receipt
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 22 — Setup: the cashier sells milk + bread, cash (a two-line order)
# @name twoLineOrder
# Expected: 201 — totalKobo 1600000
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}",
      "quantity": 1
    },
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listingBread.response.body.publicId}}",
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ],
  "paymentMethod": "CASH"
}

### 23 — A counter-settled refund WITHOUT a return method
# Expected: 400 — "Record how the money went back: CASH or BANK_TRANSFER."
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{twoLineOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ]
}

### 24 — The manager refunds the milk line, bank transfer back to the customer
# Expected: 201 — totalKobo 1200000, displayTotal "₦12000.00", returnMethod "BANK_TRANSFER"
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{twoLineOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "BANK_TRANSFER"
}

### 25 — Machine receipt after a PARTIAL refund: still PAID
# Expected: 200 — status "PAID", refunds array with ONE entry
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{twoLineOrder.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 26 — The manager refunds the bread line, spoilt — DAMAGED
# Expected: 201 — totalKobo 400000, displayTotal "₦4000.00", line disposition "DAMAGED".
# No stock back, no movement row (the shelf keeps 9 milk / 9 bread).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{twoLineOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listingBread.response.body.publicId}}",
      "disposition": "DAMAGED"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 27 — Machine receipt after EVERY line is refunded: derived REFUNDED
# Expected: 200 — status "REFUNDED", statusReason "FULLY_REFUNDED",
# refunds array with TWO entries (chronological)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{twoLineOrder.response.body.publicId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 28 — The SELLING cashier refunds their own sale — refused
# Expected: 403 — order-access-denied (sales and corrections stay separated)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listingBread.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 29 — The sales attendant refunds — also refused
# Expected: 403 — order-access-denied
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{twoLineOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{attendantLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listingBread.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 30 — An order that never existed
# Expected: 404 — order-not-found
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 31 — An empty line list refunds nothing
# Expected: 400
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 32 — A product the order never carried
# Expected: 400 — "Product ... is not on this order."
# (bread was never on the one-line cashOrder)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listingBread.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 33 — Setup: the cashier opens a bank-transfer slip via Paystack
# @name gatewaySlip
# Expected: 202 — status PENDING_PAYMENT
# (Without PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY in .env: 400 payment-provider-not-configured)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}",
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ],
  "paymentMethod": "TRANSFER",
  "paymentProvider": "PAYSTACK"
}

### 34 — Refunding a slip that was never paid — no money moved
# Expected: 409 — order-not-refundable (this one is PENDING_PAYMENT)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{gatewaySlip.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ]
}

### 35 — Gateway refund (MANUAL — needs a human): the reversal
# MANUAL STEP FIRST: complete the sandbox payment in the
# paystack.authorizationUrl from case 33, then send
# payments/verify.http for this slip so it settles PAID.
# THEN this request:
# Expected: 201 — providerRefundReference present (the provider's
# refund reference), displayTotal present, returnMethod ABSENT (the reversal IS the return).
# No returnMethod on a gateway order is REQUIRED — sending one is a 400.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{gatewaySlip.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ]
}

### 36 — Anonymous refund — no token at all
# Expected: 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{cashOrder.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "returnMethod": "CASH"
}

### 37 — Setup: the cashier opens a bank-transfer slip via Monnify
# @name monnifySlip
# Expected: 202 — status PENDING_PAYMENT, monnify VA account in the body
# (Without MONNIFY keys in .env: 400 payment-provider-not-configured)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{cashierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}",
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ],
  "paymentMethod": "TRANSFER",
  "paymentProvider": "MONNIFY"
}

### 38 — Refunding a Monnify slip that was never paid — no money moved
# Expected: 409 — order-not-refundable (PENDING_PAYMENT; the status gate
# fires BEFORE the destination-account rule, on purpose)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{monnifySlip.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ]
}

### 39 — Monnify refund WITHOUT the destination pair: 400 (MANUAL — needs a human)
# MANUAL STEP FIRST: complete the sandbox payment on the monnifySlip VA
# account (number + expiry from the case-37 response), then send
# payments/verify.http for this slip so it settles PAID.
# THEN send THIS request first:
# Expected: 400 — "Monnify refunds need the customer's destination account
# and bank code." A 400 records nothing — the line stays refundable for case 40.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{monnifySlip.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ]
}

### 40 — Gateway refund via Monnify (MANUAL — needs a human): the reversal
# Send AFTER case 39 returned its 400. Monnify refunds the VA payment to the
# customer's REGULAR account — the destination pair is REQUIRED here.
# Expected: 201 — providerRefundReference present, displayTotal present,
# returnMethod ABSENT (the reversal IS the return).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders/{{monnifySlip.response.body.publicId}}/refunds
Authorization: Bearer {{managerLogin.response.body.accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lines": [
    {
      "productPublicId": "{{listing.response.body.publicId}}"
    }
  ],
  "destinationAccount": "0123456789",
  "destinationBankCode": "032"
}
